  "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(1)=2$ and $g(1)=1.$\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
